<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William FitzOsbern</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1020–<a href="/years/1071">1071</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as William fitz Osbern</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William FitzOsbern was a Norman lord, kinsman and close adviser of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>, and one of the planners of the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1066. Made Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hereford">Hereford</a>, he built castles at Chepstow and elsewhere to push Norman power into south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. He was killed at the Battle of Cassel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> in 1071.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-fitzosbern.jpg" alt="William FitzOsbern" width="320" height="381" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jakednb · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_FitzOsbern_Coat_of_Arms.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>William FitzOsbern was the son of Osbern the Steward, a nephew of Duchess Gunnor, wife of Duke Richard I of Normandy. Osbern served as steward to his cousin Duke Robert I and became one of the guardians of the young Duke William. Through his marriage to Emma, daughter of Count Rodulf of Ivry, Osbern acquired a large property in central Normandy, including the honours of Pacy and Breteuil. William was probably raised at the ducal court and, like his father, became one of the duke's stewards. With his wife Adeliza de Tosny he founded Lyre Abbey and later Cormeilles Abbey.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He was among the earliest advocates of the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and tradition holds that at the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lillebonne">Lillebonne</a> he convinced the doubters among the Norman barons. His younger brother Osbern FitzOsbern, a chaplain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a> who held the church of Bosham in Sussex, was well placed to pass on news from England. FitzOsbern is one of the few proven companions of the Conqueror known to have fought at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings">Battle of Hastings</a> in 1066.</p><p>Created Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hereford">Hereford</a> in 1067, he held major lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucestershire">Gloucestershire</a>, Herefordshire, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxfordshire">Oxfordshire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isle-of-wight">Isle of Wight</a>, and in the summer of 1067 governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> with Bishop Odo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux">Bayeux</a> while the king was in Normandy. In 1069 he kept the peace at York and probably played a major part in crushing the revolt of Eadric the Wild, before pushing west to begin the Norman conquest of the Welsh kingdom of Gwent. Castles attributed to him include Carisbrooke, his chief residence, Wigmore, Clifford, Berkeley and Monmouth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1070 Count Baldwin VI of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> died, leaving his county and young sons to his widow Richilde, Countess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a> and Hainaut, whose rule was challenged by her brother-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-i-count-of-flanders">Robert the Frisian</a>. She offered herself in marriage to FitzOsbern, who hurried to Flanders with his army in the hope of becoming count, and he was killed at Cassel on 22 February 1071. His son William of Breteuil succeeded him in Normandy, and Roger de Breteuil became 2nd Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hereford">Hereford</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William FitzOsbern, 1st Earl of Hereford” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Norman Conquest <b>William FitzOsbern</b> built Chepstow Castle from 1067 and pushed west.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-gwent">Kingdom of Gwent</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Normandy, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Cassel, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.12,-0.44&amp;year=1053" aria-label="Normandy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4540 450 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William FitzOsbern"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4990 720h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4990 720h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.12,-0.44&amp;year=1053">What was here in 1053? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
